+## Resolution
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A coordinator resolves outstanding competing revisions through the review-outcome operation. The operation records the coordinator's identity, the contributing revision IDs, and the outcome, and establishes the visit's current tip. Revisions not carried into the current tip remain in the visit's history and are never discarded.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency mechanism | Whole-visit revisions: each submission is an intact revision naming its base; concurrent submissions become competing revisions resolved by a coordinator | Field-aware three-way merge (combine disjoint field edits, review overlaps) | Field names do not reveal which observations are scientifically related, so an automatic merge could join a species identification from one worker with a cover estimate from another and produce a record no worker submitted, with no coordinator noticing. Whole-visit revisions keep each submitted visit intact and the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable. They enlarge the coordinator review queue because even unrelated-field edits compete; that cost is accepted to avoid maintained merge rules. |
+| Submission shape | A complete visit per submission | Structured field changes / deltas | A whole visit preserves the worker's submission as a unit and needs no per-field merge logic. |
+| Idempotency | Idempotency key per submission | Deduplication by payload hash | Workers retry after offline connection failures; the key returns the original acceptance without creating a second stored edit. |
 
